WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:14.441 --> 00:00:16.932
My grandma is my queen.

00:00:16.932 --> 00:00:24.905
Engineer by education, she started her
career as a rocket scientist at 22.

00:00:24.905 --> 00:00:30.719
At 46, she became director
of a summer camp for kids.

00:00:30.719 --> 00:00:34.989
At 69, she left this position, no, not for

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:39.041
retirement, but
to become an HR manager for

00:00:39.041 --> 00:00:43.875
the rocket science company
she used to work before.

00:00:43.875 --> 00:00:47.827
She retired only 13 years later, at 82,

00:00:47.827 --> 00:00:52.929
beloved by everyone who was
lucky enough to work with her.

00:00:52.929 --> 00:00:56.522
She's now 88, and my god,
you should see the flowers and

00:00:56.522 --> 00:00:59.234
veggies she's growing
at her country house.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.089
My grandma's career path,

00:01:04.089 --> 00:01:09.104
with it's multiple pivots into her 80s,
is extraordinary for

00:01:09.104 --> 00:01:14.317
her time, but she's a forerunner
of how a career should look like.

00:01:14.317 --> 00:01:19.363
Nowadays we have a good chance
to live until 100 and beyond.

00:01:19.363 --> 00:01:23.958
We are ageing so much healthfully,
this is exciting.

00:01:23.958 --> 00:01:28.643
We can accomplish so
much more during our lifetime.

00:01:28.643 --> 00:01:33.387
But to take advantage of
this gift of a long life,

00:01:33.387 --> 00:01:37.226
we must prepare for career longevity.

00:01:37.226 --> 00:01:42.093
I want to offer you three
ideas of how to do this.

00:01:42.093 --> 00:01:50.712
Number one, start thinking of your career
as a portfolio, not as a straight line.

00:01:50.712 --> 00:01:58.015
For many years, we have thought of a
successful career as of something linear,

00:01:58.015 --> 00:02:02.711
it has a beginning, a peak and
an assumed decline.

00:02:02.711 --> 00:02:05.183
The idea of career longevity
brings a portfolio approach.

00:02:05.183 --> 00:02:10.086
Plan your career as a set of activities,
project,

00:02:10.086 --> 00:02:13.171
directions, like my grandma.

00:02:13.171 --> 00:02:19.803
Portfolio approach guarantees
more stability and more fun.

00:02:19.803 --> 00:02:26.312
Stability is achieved by diversifying
your professional capabilities.

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:31.539
The world is changing so quickly,
professions rise and die, so do companies.

00:02:31.539 --> 00:02:37.196
Just as in investing,
betting on just one thing is risky.

00:02:37.196 --> 00:02:42.510
So diversify your career portfolio
to gain more stability and

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:46.093
lessen the risk of becoming irrelevant.

00:02:46.093 --> 00:02:50.014
But also look at it from the fun angle.

00:02:50.014 --> 00:02:54.678
You have gained an additional
30 years of life.

00:02:54.678 --> 00:02:59.984
Some of that should definitely go
to doing something interesting.

00:02:59.984 --> 00:03:04.017
Ask yourself, what else do I want to do?

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:09.416
Create a wish list and
start working on it now.

00:03:09.416 --> 00:03:14.049
I try to keep the growing list for myself.

00:03:14.049 --> 00:03:18.214
Start my own company,
do something for climate change,

00:03:18.214 --> 00:03:22.219
become a lecturer at Stanford,
get a medical degree.

00:03:24.396 --> 00:03:31.501
Suggestion number two is to
train yourself to be flexible.

00:03:31.501 --> 00:03:36.460
It is very scary to adapt to rapid
changes, if you're not used to this.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:43.408
Nowadays, the key competitive advantage
is how quickly one can handle

00:03:43.408 --> 00:03:48.290
the uncertainty, adapt to new realities,
and learn new skills.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:53.923
My practical advice to anyone,
regardless of age,

00:03:53.923 --> 00:03:59.623
is to train yourself to be flexible
by studying something challenging.

00:03:59.623 --> 00:04:04.562
Programming, a new foreign language, arts,
somethings that your brain doesn't like.

00:04:04.562 --> 00:04:09.284
For me, it became math.

00:04:09.284 --> 00:04:14.520
The last couple of years I've made myself
working on math problems every week.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.360
In the beginning, it was torture,
but now it's fun.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.290
Suggestion number three is more of an ask.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:29.470
Shape the future by building
multi-generational teams.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:35.060
Multi-generational teams
are rare in business now.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:38.105
Younger people often resist
older employees, and

00:04:38.105 --> 00:04:42.230
older employees sometimes struggle
to work within younger groups.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:44.372
But actually,

00:04:44.372 --> 00:04:49.108
research by Laura Carstensen at
the Stanford Center on Longevity indicates

00:04:49.108 --> 00:04:54.370
that multi-generational teams are more
successful than only generational ones.

00:04:56.170 --> 00:05:00.034
One of my best colleagues at
PWC was a retired partner,

00:05:00.034 --> 00:05:04.662
who continued to engage with
student events well into his 60s.

00:05:04.662 --> 00:05:09.289
It was amazing having someone
like him sharing his wisdom.

00:05:09.289 --> 00:05:14.020
His energy was infectious, and I would
work with him again in a heartbeat.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:20.280
I'm sure the people listening to me
today are leaders who are hiring.

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:27.850
My prime message to you is not to be
afraid to build multi-generational teams.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:31.199
It is strategic for your business and

00:05:31.199 --> 00:05:34.160
contributes to your
personal career longevity.

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:39.680
We can confidently say that
people in their 30s or

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.336
40s today will work another 40 or
50 years.

00:05:43.336 --> 00:05:48.613
While we consider a career was multiple
pivots, there are basic skills like

00:05:48.613 --> 00:05:53.653
communicating and collaborating with
people that should be mastered.

00:05:53.653 --> 00:05:59.472
But this is an exciting, lifelong journey,
and you can start it today.

00:05:59.472 --> 00:06:03.210
[MUSIC]

